# Gotchas

Real mistakes this project's own Flask-SQLAlchemy templates hit while
being built — not hypothetical.

## `db.create_all()` must never run unconditionally when migrations are on

This was a real bug in this project's own templates: `init_db()` used
to call `db.create_all()` on every app boot regardless of whether
migrations were enabled. The consequence wasn't an obvious crash — it
was worse, a silent trap:

1. `create_app()` runs, `init_db()` calls `db.create_all()`, and the
   tables get created directly from the current models — outside any
   migration history.
2. Later, someone runs `flask db migrate -m "..."` for the first time.
   Alembic (which `flask-migrate` uses under the hood) diffs the models
   against the *live database* — but the tables already exist and
   already match the models exactly, because `create_all()` made them.
   Autogenerate reports **"No changes in schema detected"** and writes
   an empty migration, forever — there's no real migration history
   capturing how the schema came to look the way it does.
3. On a genuinely fresh database (a new environment, a teammate's
   machine), `flask db upgrade` would eventually try to create those
   same tables again — and since `alembic_version` (the table Alembic
   uses to track which migrations have run) was never stamped, nothing
   in the setup tells you the schema was created out-of-band.

{% if migrations %}
This project has `migrations=true`, so the fix is already in place:
`core/db.py`'s `init_db()` only calls `db.create_all()` when
`testing=True`:

```python
db.init_app(app)
migrate.init_app(app, db)
if testing:
    with app.app_context():
        db.create_all()
```

The test suite's in-memory SQLite database has no migration history to
replay against and is thrown away after the run, so it still needs a
direct `create_all()`. Real usage must **not** take this path — schema
comes from `flask db upgrade` instead (see the `flask-migrate` skill,
and this project's README's "Migrations" section for the exact
commands). If you're seeing "table does not exist" errors when running
the app for real, that's the fix: run the migration, don't add an
unconditional `create_all()` back into `init_db()` to make the error go
away.
{% else %}
This project currently has `migrations=false`, so `init_db()` calls
`db.create_all()` unconditionally on every startup — which is fine
*only* because there's no migration history for it to fall out of
sync with:

```python
db.init_app(app)
with app.app_context():
    db.create_all()
```

If you turn `migrations` on later (re-run conjure, or add Flask-Migrate
by hand), this call must become conditional on `testing` — see the
`if migrations` branch of this doc for the exact shape. `create_all()`
and a real migration history must never coexist unconditionally; the
moment both are active, autogenerate permanently reports "No changes
in schema detected" instead of capturing the real schema, because the
tables it's diffing against already match the models before any
migration ever ran.
{% endif %}

This is exactly why `init_db(app, *, testing: bool = False)` takes that
keyword — trace it back to `main.py`'s
`create_app(*, database_url=None, testing=False)`, which calls
`init_db(app, testing=testing)`. `tests/conftest.py` is the only
caller that passes `testing=True`.

## `db.session` needs an application context

Every `db.session.execute(...)`/`db.session.add(...)`/`db.session.commit()`
call in `routes/items.py` runs inside a Flask route handler, which
always has an active application (and request) context — so this is
easy to never think about. It stops being automatic the moment you
write code that touches `db.session` *outside* a request: a one-off
CLI script, a `flask shell` session, a scheduled job, a Celery task
body. Doing that without first entering a context raises:

```
RuntimeError: Working outside of application context.
```

Wrap the call in `with app.app_context(): ...` (import `create_app`,
build an app, then enter the context) the same way `init_db()` itself
does around its own `db.create_all()` call. There is no way to safely
skip this — `db.session` is a context-local proxy, not a plain object,
by design (it's what lets the same `db` singleton serve concurrent
requests without them stepping on each other's session).

## Query syntax is a `sqlalchemy` skill concern, not this one

`db.select(...)`, `.scalars()`, filtering, joins, and eager-loading
(`db.relationship(...)`, `selectinload`, ...) are plain SQLAlchemy —
Flask-SQLAlchemy only re-exports them off `db` for convenience. If
you're debugging a query itself rather than how it's wired into Flask,
see the `sqlalchemy` skill; duplicating that material here would just
drift out of sync with it.
